    ":question/id": "38246601-aa92-5ddc-abf5-04a09da1de9a",
    ":question/problem": "![Upper semicircle with diameter endpoints A(-1,1) and B(5,1)](images/de/dec59c468bd95a9e3e641209b688a1fa0c0cb6fa0d078730d87653820e2cce7e.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the upper semicircular arc with diameter $AB$, traversed once from $B$ to $A$. The dashed diameter is a guide, not part of the path.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of the diameter are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(5,1)$. Since they have the same $y$-coordinate,\n\n$$\nd=AB=5-(-1)=6,\\qquad r=\\frac{d}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nThe length of the semicircular arc is half the circumference:\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi r)=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe diameter is not traversed. Reversing direction would leave the arc length unchanged."
  },
