    ":question/id": "527f107a-9fa1-58fb-8948-3a1234bf8e02",
    ":question/problem": "![Clockwise circle with diagonal diameter from A(-2,-1) to B(2,3)](images/8e/8e7bb5ef8527a97d61481cd545dbbeb4a30ef383b8f8fa273aee9061d1472f2d.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle with diameter $AB$, traversed once clockwise. The dashed segment $AB$ is a guide, not part of the path.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nFirst find the diameter using the endpoint coordinates. The right triangle below is an auxiliary construction, not an additional path:\n\n![Right triangle with horizontal and vertical legs of length 4 along diameter AB](images/df/df4ccec99dcb2e5bc87533203c10475d5052c1dbc46ee699c4ce5f7d3695e7fb.png)\n\nThe coordinate differences are $2-(-2)=4$ and $3-(-1)=4$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd=AB&=\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^2+(3-(-1))^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4^2+4^2}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA full traversal has length equal to the circumference:\n\n$$\nL=\\pi d=4\\pi\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=4\\pi\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nA closed path has zero net displacement, but its arc length is positive. Clockwise traversal does not make an integral with respect to $s$ negative."
  }
